/**
 * German for the Zhizn v Rossii practice questions.
 *
 * The last and largest of the seven banks. For the other six a reader who
 * loses the thread can still guess at the question from the shape of the
 * words; in front of Cyrillic they cannot, so an untranslated bank here was
 * not a rough patch but a wall. That is why ZHIZN_V_ROSSII_DE covers the
 * quizzes inside the lessons and the three older tables do not — and why the
 * practice tab and the exam, which draw on this separate pool, were the last
 * place in the course still asking in a script the reader cannot read.
 *
 * Keyed on the RUSSIAN of ruQuestionBank.ts, exactly as it stands there. Every
 * key was extracted from the built module and paired back by position, never
 * retyped: one wrong character — an и for a й, a ё written as е — and the
 * lookup misses in silence, so the marker disappears and the reader is told
 * there is nothing for a question that was in fact translated.
 *
 * WHAT STAYS RUSSIAN, and it follows ZHIZN_V_ROSSII_DE word for word, because
 * a reader meets both tables in the same course and one term glossed two ways
 * reads like two things. Proper names in their German conventional form:
 * Duma, Kreml, Rubel, Transsib. Terms the exam asks for that have no German
 * equivalent keep the abbreviation and gain the meaning beside it: EGE, MROT,
 * SNILS, SATO. Everyday words the course already teaches stay as it teaches
 * them: Propiska against Meldung, Datscha, Banja, Wenik, Marschrutka,
 * Elektritschka, Borschtsch, Schtschi, Snegurotschka.
 *
 * Strings another German table already answers are deliberately absent. Every
 * table for one target is spread into a single object, so a second entry for
 * the same key would be a silent collision, and the older table would decide
 * the wording for both. check-question-bank-translation counts what a reader
 * reaches rather than what this file holds.
 */
